WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.500
Johnny: You are tearing me apart, Lisa!

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.000
When you think of "it's so bad, it's good",

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.000
you probably think of some movie with laughably horrible acting and writing.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.090
A movie that's so bad,

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.000
it becomes really fun to watch.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.800
You watch it and make fun of it with your friends because such a bad movie takes itself so seriously.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.300
"So bad, it's good" movies go as far back as the B movies of the 40s and 50s.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.500
The concept has always been the same.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:22.700
They're guilty pleasure movies

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.300
where you enjoy the movie because of its excessive use of:

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.840
movie tropes, poor over-the-top acting, and terrible camerawork,

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:29.640
and it makes sense

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.220
It's not hard to see the similar laughability of Robot Monster in 1953 and Sharknado (2013).

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.560
The problem is that analysis of "so bad, it's good" usually stops there,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.320
but I think you can go much deeper than that

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.040
I want to talk about what's at the core of this phenomenon,

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:48.663
and make the case that the techniques of "so bad, it's good" are present in tons of critically acclaimed movies,

00:00:48.663 --> 00:00:50.400
and almost all the time, It's done on purpose.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.380
So let me explain.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:52.080
Erin: Michael.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:54.460
Michael: Walk with me.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.180
"So bad, it's good" movies are an extreme version of the idea that

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:02.160
audiences enjoy theatrical and over-the-top movies that still take themselves seriously,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.020
This concept is called "camp."

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.100
And it's another tool at the disposal of a filmmaker.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.800
When you think of campy movies you may think they're always made unintentionally.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.660
Like Tommy Wiseau trying to film a serious movie and accidentally creating the ultimate camp classic.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.160
But that's actually not the case.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:21.640
Directors constantly pretend to take their dumb movie seriously to create a whole new level of entertainment.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.960
Anthony Ferrante: But if everybody is earnest and serious and treating it like it's, you know, a regular earthquake,

00:01:25.979 --> 00:01:28.169
or it's you know an alien invasion or whatever it is,

00:01:28.169 --> 00:01:35.580
then, then that sincerity kind of translates, and then you can go even further with your villain, which is the Sharknado.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:36.660
You can do crazy stuff.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.140
Kimberly Q: And that makes it even more funny.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.800
Camp isn't necessarily a genre of movies.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.660
Some movies can have elements of camp without exclusively considering themselves "so bad, it's good."

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.260
Maybe the perfect balance of camp and sincerity is the Princess Bride (1987).

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.560
The movie is actually smartly written and well executed

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.440
while also having a ton of campy and corny moments

00:01:55.149 --> 00:01:58.180
Montoya: You seem a decent fellow. . . I hate to kill you.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.500
Man in Black: You seem a decent fellow. . . I hate to die.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.800
The swordfight blurs the line between cartoonish and serious.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.000
The stakes are high and the scene is expertly choreographed

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.100
but they also do random flips.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.080
Camp makes the Princess Bride fun to watch without making it a movie people make fun of.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:12.780
It's over the top

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.660
but never too much so and it always treats itself seriously.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.870
Camp can actually sometimes save a movie.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:22.080
Johnny Depp's performance turns the Pirates of the Caribbean from a derivative action flick

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.940
to a charming and hilarious franchise.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:27.640
And it's because of his campy, exaggerated, but grounded performance.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.380
Norrington: You are without a doubt, the worst pirate I've ever heard of.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.820
Sparrow: But you have heard of me.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.880
Wes Anderson is a champion of camp.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.180
His set pieces are famously symmetrical to the point of impossibility.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.160
The camera constantly spins from one character to another.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.640
He loves using outdated cartoonish special effects

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.960
And he awkwardly cuts conversations at the end of each sentence.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.580
Sam: Why do you consider me your enemy?

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.800
Redford: Because your girlfriend stabbed me in the back with lefty scissors.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.700
Sam: She's my life now.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.020
Redford: Congratulations.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:04.200
Anderson creates charming and critically acclaimed films despite breaking countless film conventions.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.400
Campy film techniques give movies that extra flair that makes them fun and devastatingly rewatchable.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.940
Campy filmmakers strike the balance between the impossible and the realistic.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:16.680
So that movies have elements of camp while also having just a hint of seriousness

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:19.140
So they don't seem like they're trying too hard.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.889
Anderson: For me, you know, Rafe is playing a real person and making him a real person and

00:03:24.859 --> 00:03:28.809
Even though the context is this sort of invented country in this

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:34.869
invented version of history and and so on I sort of feel like we just try to make it as realistic as it can be

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:40.449
Given given what it is exaggerated and campy filmmaking happens all the time so this

00:03:43.639 --> 00:03:45.639
Becomes this

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:51.709
And this

00:03:53.829 --> 00:03:55.829
Becomes this

00:03:58.569 --> 00:04:00.034
Quentin Tarantino loves camp.

00:04:00.034 --> 00:04:04.300
If you have a villain, make it a school girl and give her a flail as a weapon.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:10.500
His love for the theatrical and exaggerated shows in the fact that in all of his movies characters pretend to be somebody they're not.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.830
Winnfield: Come on, let's get into character.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:17.300
The result is hilariously over-the-top, but serious moments of poor or flashy acting.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:20.620
Landa: Arrivederci.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:22.040
Raine: A-reev-a-durchey.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.680
Camp is also extremely common in dialogue; so many movies have characters say things nobody says in real life...

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.580
Rollo: This is one doodle that can't be undid homeskillet.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.940
...but that doesn't make the dialogue bad.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:33.780
Well sometimes it does...

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.760
Johnny: I did not hit her. It's not true. It's bullshit. I did not hit her. I did not!

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:40.480
Oh, hi Mark!

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.040
...but it's also a way to make movies fun.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.680
Aaron Sorkin is one of the best screenwriters in cinema, and he loves campy dialogue.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.980
In many scenes he favors exaggerated dialogue over what someone would actually say.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:51.980
Summers: Ann.

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:52.860
Ann: Yes sir?

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.700
Summers: Punch me in the face. Go ahead.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:59.640
Aaron Sorkin's famous "walk and talk" style from West Wing feels extremely theatrical and contrived

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.980
It's been reused to the point that it's been parodied in just about every TV sitcom.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.282
He even parodies it himself.

00:05:05.282 --> 00:05:05.840
Sorkin: Walk with me

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:07.840
I'm Aaron Sorkin.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.220
His snappy dialogue also gets reused to an amazing degree.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:13.620
This is you and me talking Dana. I'm not other people

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:14.726
I'm not other people.

00:05:14.726 --> 00:05:15.780
I'm not other people.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.420
I'm not other people!

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:18.570
Don't talk to me like I'm other people.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:19.840
But it's not bad writing.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:25.840
It actually takes a very talented writer to create a style that's so easy to parody, but remains fascinating to watch.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.470
He's not technically original, but he still keeps it fresh.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:33.119
We can feel his classic style, but we don't loathe it. We embrace it. To a crazy amount actually.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.840
It's almost worshipped to the point where they make actors recreate Sorkin style on talk shows

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.280
Camp is letting movies be movies and it's a concept we feel everywhere.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:51.620
Basically whenever a movie does something over-the-top that would never happen in real life, but the movie still executes it sincerely

00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:59.500
Impressive clergyman: "Mawwiage..."

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:04.180
And "The Room" fits quite nicely into these concepts of Exaggerated and entertaining filmmaking.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:09.100
What makes the movie stand out is that it's one of the few campy movies that took itself completely seriously

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:12.177
without having any idea just how over-the-top it was.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.720
It's still camp, but it's so unique because of how blissfully unaware it is.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:16.660
Johnny: Hi doggy.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:17.860
Clerk: You're my favorite customer.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:19.280
Johnny: Thanks a lot! Bye!

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:21.820
And this brings me to the most important component of camp:

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:25.580
Camp defies binary thinking in the movies because it's both bad and good.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:28.650
It objectively breaks many of the principles of filmmaking:

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:35.460
Unrealistic acting, cheesy dialogue, and extreme filming techniques are almost always frowned upon, but the crazy thing is camp is not bad.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:40.860
Sometimes it's actually extremely entertaining, perhaps the most entertaining kind of films that exist.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:44.160
Because many directors can be serious and many directors can be funny

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.320
But not many can be so serious it's funny

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:50.140
I embrace camp because movies shouldn't always play it straight if they don't want to.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:54.180
On the contrary, camp lets movies be what they were meant to be: Entertaining.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:57.400
So the next time you watch a movie don't just consider whether it's good or bad

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:02.400
Consider if you like it for its guilty pleasures. Consider that it's so bad, but that's not a bad thing

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.840
It's actually a great thing. It's so good that it's bad.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:07.820
Lisa: Do you want me to order a pizza?

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:09.620
Johnny: Whatever. I don't care.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:10.880
Lisa: I already ordered a pizza.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:12.863
Johnny: You think about everything.

00:07:12.863 --> 00:07:13.940
Thanks for watching

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.320
Outro: Here it is, a groove slightly transformed!

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.220
Just a bit of a break from the norm.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:22.700
Just a little something to break the monotony of all that hard... [video end]

